408→                           These always exist if $n$ is a prime power. This conjecture has been proved for $n\leq 11$, but it is open whether there exists a projective plane of order $12$.<br><br>Bruck and Ryser <a href="#cite-BrRy49-723" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('BrRy49','723'); return false;">[BrRy49]</a> have <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bruck%E2%80%93Ryser%E2%80%93Chowla_theorem" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer ugc" target="_blank">proved</a> that if $n\equiv 1\pmod{4}$ or $n\equiv 2\pmod{4}$ then $n$ must be the sum of two squares. For example, this rules out $n=6$ or $n=14$. The case $n=10$ was ruled out by computer search <a href="#cite-La97-723" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('La97','723'); return false;">[La97]</a>.
